            "- **\"It writes everything, I just vibe.\"** That is the demo, not the practice: unreviewed generation accumulates defects at generation speed. The loop's stages are not optional steps; they are what makes the output yours.\n- **Skipping verification because it looks right.** Plausible is the model's native output; correctness is established by tests, diff-reading and edges — every time, \"one-liners\" included (Tuesday's bug).\n- **Accepting tests that assert nothing.** Read the generated tests: do they check the acceptance criteria, or just that the function returns something? Tautological tests launder unverified code.\n- **No grounding in the project.** Without a context file of conventions, commands and rules, output is generic-idiomatic — plausible near-duplicates of what you already have. Grounding is the highest-leverage setup work.\n- **Monolithic prompts / giant single-shot generations.** Unauditable. Small, reviewable steps — the same economics as the PR-size rule.\n- **Treating integration as someone else's problem.** Generated modules arrive unaware of your error envelope, auth layer and schemas. The integration pass is the human job that makes a system.\n- **Letting agents near secrets or production.** Autonomy scales capability *and* blast radius. Sandboxes, no credentials, read-only where possible — the container lesson as the agent's cage.\n- **Out-of-date API knowledge.** Generated code targeting last year's library version fails in instructive ways (method exists in no version). Pin your versions in context; verify against current docs.\n- **\"The AI made me do it.\"** The human approves every shipped line — responsibility does not transfer because generation was fast. That is not a moral position; it is what ownership of the artefact means.\n- **Skipping the reflection stage.** Every violation fed back (context file, test, lint rule) raises the floor; without it you pay for the same lesson repeatedly.\n- **Confusing fluency with correctness.** A confident, well-formatted explanation of wrong code is the normal failure mode — pair prose confidence with a test that would catch it.\n- **Thinking prior programming knowledge is optional.** The spec, the review and the debugging all draw on it — the loop needs someone who can tell a good plan from a plausible one. The skills moved; they did not vanish.\n- **Never measuring.** Lines shipped, bugs escaped, context additions, time saved — a few numbers keep the practice honest and reveal when \"saving time\" is actually hiding review debt.\n- **Code slop as the end state.** Fifty generations with no refactoring passes is a codebase nobody can change. Schedule the readability lesson's refactors; green tests are the licence.",
