          heading: "Common mistakes and misconceptions",
          kind: "warning",
          body:
            "- **Polishing by eye.** Gradients over audits. Every item on the real list is instrument-checkable; run the instruments and let taste have the remaining minutes.\n- **Skipping the direct-curl authorisation pass.** UI-hidden ≠ server-checked — the security lessons' first rule, forgotten the moment the UI \"looks locked\".\n- **Secrets sweep done once, never automated.** Today's grep becomes tomorrow's CI secret scan; manual sweeps decay, pipelines don't.\n- **Lighthouse run but not gated.** A score nobody blocks on is a poster; wire the budget into CI so regressions fail the build.\n- **Deployed from the laptop.** The URL must come from the commit — otherwise your rollback pointer points at nothing.\n- **No smoke on the real phone, over mobile data.** The lesson's own device class (keyboard, viewport, weight) only appears there.\n- **Launch notes without a \"next session starts here\".** Future-you (or an AI session) pays a full re-entry tax every time; five lines remove it.\n- **No maintenance date.** \"I'll update it when needed\" fails because \"needed\" arrives as an outage. Calendar it at 30 minutes; CI does the rest.\n- **Regenerating against stale context.** Next month's session without a current context file re-solves solved problems and re-breaks fixed ones — the context file is the project's memory, feed it every reflection.\n- **Loop skipped because \"I know this code now\".** You know the parts you wrote; most of it you didn't. Criteria and reading shrink with ceremony, never to zero.\n- **Code slop never repaid.** One refactor slice per maintenance date, under green tests — otherwise the project reaches the point where no one (including you-with-an-AI) can safely change it.\n- **Never looking at real use.** A counter, a contact link, a glance a month — zero-observability projects cannot learn from their users, and users find device classes you do not own.\n- **Undecided fate = ambient guilt.** Portfolio, tool, or archive — declare it in the launch notes and revisit annually. An honest archived project is finished; a half-live one never is.\n- **Version chaos.** Tag releases on behaviour change; chores don't need tags — but *some* immutable pointer per shipped behaviour, or rollback is archaeology.",
